A skew lattice is an algebra $(S; \wedge, \vee)$ where $\wedge$ and $\vee$ are each associative, idempotent binary operations satisfying the absorption identities $x \wedge (x \vee y) = x = x \vee (x \wedge y)$ and $(x \wedge y) \vee y = y = (x \vee y) \wedge y$. On any skew lattice, Green's relation $\mathcal{D}$ is a congruence, and $S/\mathcal{D}$ is the maximal lattice image of $S$. A skew lattice is called primitive if it has exactly two $\mathcal{D}$-classes (one comparable pair in $S/\mathcal{D}$). 

Recall that a skew lattice is:
- distributive if it satisfies $x \wedge (y \vee z) \wedge x = (x \wedge y \wedge x) \vee (x \wedge z \wedge x)$ and the dual identity;
- cancellative if $x \vee y = x \vee z$ and $x \wedge y = x \wedge z$ imply $y = z$, and dually;
- symmetric if $x \vee y = y \vee x$ iff $x \wedge y = y \wedge x$;
- categorical if nonempty composites of coset bijections between $\mathcal{D}$-classes are coset bijections;
- strictly categorical if, given $\mathcal{D}$-classes $A > B > C$, every coset bijection from $A$ to $C$ factors uniquely through $B$.

Let $\mathbf{P}$ denote the subvariety of the variety of skew lattices generated by all primitive skew lattices. It is known that $\mathbf{P}$ is distributive, cancellative, and strictly categorical. 

(a) Determine all further equational/structural properties characterizing $\mathbf{P}$. 
(b) Provide necessary and sufficient conditions on a skew lattice $S$ for $S \in \mathbf{P}$.

